WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389
Layered defenses against test tampering.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:06.403
No single control is enough, because each covers a different path.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:07.557
Stack them.

4
00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:08.840
Instructions.

5
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:09.888
CLAUDE.

6
00:00:10.168 --> 00:00:16.803
md and the prompt say: don’t edit, skip, or delete tests; if a test seems wrong, stop and explain.

7
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.347
Necessary, and the research above shows it isn’t sufficient.

8
00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:21.757
Permission rules.

9
00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:26.347
A deny rule such as Edit(/tests/**) in.

10
00:00:26.627 --> 00:00:27.907
claude/settings.

11
00:00:28.187 --> 00:00:29.125
json.

12
00:00:29.405 --> 00:00:43.485
The permissions docs say Read and Edit deny rules apply to Claude’s file tools and to file commands Claude Code recognizes in Bash ( sed, tee, redirections), but not to arbitrary scripts that open files themselves.

13
00:00:43.765 --> 00:00:50.784
A PreToolUse hook that blocks Edit and Write on test paths, with a message telling the agent what to do instead.

14
00:00:51.064 --> 00:00:54.712
Same coverage gap: it sees only the tools its matcher names.

15
00:00:54.992 --> 00:01:00.475
The sandbox, for OS-level enforcement that applies to every process, including scripts.

16
00:01:00.755 --> 00:01:05.064
Commit tests first, so any later change to them is a separate, visible diff.

17
00:01:05.344 --> 00:01:08.928
Holdout tests the agent never sees, run after it finishes.

18
00:01:09.208 --> 00:01:16.312
This is ImpossibleBench’s mitigation in practice, and it also catches fixes that special-case the visible tests.

19
00:01:16.592 --> 00:01:18.085
The lab’s make_workspace.

20
00:01:18.365 --> 00:01:20.093
py score does exactly this.

21
00:01:20.373 --> 00:01:21.931
CI and review rules.

22
00:01:22.211 --> 00:01:35.459
A CI step that fails if test files changed without a label or approval; GitHub’s CODEOWNERS can require a specific reviewer for tests/, and branch protection can require that review before merge.

23
00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:40.112
Layers 2 through 4 are the harness; 5 through 7 are your process.

24
00:01:40.392 --> 00:01:46.216
In a solo project, layers 1, 2, 3, 5, and 6 cost almost nothing to set up.
